# Pulse coding standards

```yaml
codingStandards:
  languages:
    Python:
      version: ">=3.11; CI and containers use 3.11"
      conventions:
        - Use type annotations for public functions, data structures, and service boundaries
        - Prefer small modules with explicit dependencies over global mutable state
        - Use UTC-aware datetimes for persisted and API timestamps
        - Keep lines at or below 100 characters except generated migration expressions
        - Use Ruff E, F, I, UP, B, and ASYNC rules from pyproject.toml
    TypeScript:
      version: "5.9.2 on Node.js 22"
      conventions:
        - Use strict TypeScript and typed API boundaries
        - Keep dashboard data access read-only and reject unsupported DTO schema versions
        - Use immutable React component inputs and bounded polling/history
    Terraform:
      version: ">=1.6.0; CI uses 1.9.8"
      conventions:
        - Format recursively and validate without a backend in CI
        - Reuse explicit existing networking and scope IAM/resources to the bounded demo
        - Never apply infrastructure or inject AWS credentials from CI

  frameworks:
    FastAPI:
      - Use application factories and lifespan hooks for resources
      - Validate request and response contracts with Pydantic
      - Keep authenticated mutation routes separate from bounded operator reads and protected commerce routes
      - Return structured, sanitized errors and truthful 503 readiness for mandatory dependency or worker failure
    SQLAlchemy:
      - Use SQLAlchemy 2 typed declarative mappings
      - Access PostgreSQL asynchronously through session factories
      - Apply schema changes through reversible Alembic migrations
      - Persist idempotency claims and complete durable intent before external mutation
    Next.js/React:
      - Use Next.js standalone output and React functional components
      - Consume pulse.snapshot.v1 through the shared explicit dashboard DTO contract
      - Keep browser configuration free of control tokens and mutation credentials
    Locust:
      - Keep scenario seeds, endpoint weights, durations, users, and evidence reproducible
      - Exercise scheduled Diwali and sudden-spike modes with bounded smoke settings in CI
    AWS/Terraform:
      - Keep dry-run client-free and live SetDesiredCapacity behind one direction/intent-aware adapter
      - Enforce application and infrastructure ceilings, resource-scoped IAM, and operator-reviewed plan/apply

  testing:
    framework: pytest + pytest-cov + Vitest/Testing Library + Locust smoke + Terraform validation
    runner: GitHub Actions plus .venv/bin/pytest and pnpm --dir dashboard test
    location: tests, dashboard/tests, load_tests, and scripts/smoke_stack.sh
    requirements:
      - New business logic requires unit tests
      - HTTP behavior, auth, DTOs, query bounds, and structured errors require API tests
      - Critical checkout protection must be tested at every shedding tier
      - Detection thresholds, cross-mode arbitration, cooldown direction, instance ceilings, dry-run, and recovery require deterministic tests
      - Database migrations must pass PostgreSQL 16 base-to-head-to-base-to-head and live repository integration
      - The production lifespan must prove initial/runtime database outage, truthful 503 degradation, worker survival, and in-process recovery
      - Backend and dashboard must consume the same versioned snapshot fixture for API-to-UI contract coverage
      - End-to-end scheduled and real-time surge modes require reproducible bounded Locust scenarios and defensible evidence
      - Terraform format/init/validate and cost/IAM assertions must pass without apply or AWS credentials
      - Secret, generated-artifact, whitespace, and conflict-marker scans must pass
    coverage:
      tool: pytest-cov
      threshold: 85

  naming:
    commits: conventional
    packages: lowercase snake_case matching the existing layout

  review:
    blockingSeverities:
      - BLOCKER
      - MAJOR
    strictReview: true
    dimensions:
      - requirements-traceability
      - architecture
      - security
      - performance
      - scalability
      - resilience
      - maintainability
      - entropy
      - cost-safety
    securityChecks:
      - no secrets or credentials in source, examples, Terraform state, or committed environment files
      - internal control endpoints require authentication
      - AWS mutations enforce dry-run and maximum capacity safeguards
      - only recovery can decrease capacity and validated scale-out bypasses provider cooldown
      - dashboard stays read-only and rejects unknown snapshot schema versions
      - persistence claims, outbox intent, retry/reconciliation, and worker readiness remain failure-safe
      - Terraform IAM is least privilege and resource scoped where AWS permits
      - do not edit .cursor kit files outside project-context

  entropy:
    enabled: true
    policy: remove-on-touch
    onChange:
      - remove unused symbols and commented-out code
      - delete superseded paths when replacing behavior
      - remove orphaned tests and configuration keys
    blockReview:
      - new commented-out code without a tracked reason
      - parallel superseded implementation left in the diff
      - placeholder implementations presented as completed features
    reference: .cursor/sdlc-system/workflow/entropy-management.md

  documentation:
    sdlcArtifacts: docs/sdlc/{workflowId}/
    projectGuide: README.md
    architecture:
      - docs/architecture.md
      - docs/state-machine.md
      - docs/demo-runbook.md
      - docs/results-template.md
    stackRules: []
```
